for over a century london has been  marking buildings where historic figures  lived with a blue plaque earlier this  month the lives of two remarkable  americans william and ellen kraft were  commemorated by the city the craft's  incredible story and their escape from  slavery is part of a change in how  london remembers its past mts taeyab  went to meet their descendants to hear  more  across london on some of its finest  streets are elegant blue plaques  commemorating prominent figures from the  past who have stayed lived or worked in  buildings around the british capital  those honored range from wartime prime  minister winston churchill to legendary  filmmaker alfred hitchcock the names on  the blue plaques are a very good measure  of whatever the society at the time  thinks is important  anna evas is a director at english  heritage the charity which has been  responsible for placing the plaques  around london for the past 135 years  when i first became responsible for the  scheme two things struck me like there  there wasn't a huge number to um  non-white people  and we were getting no nominations at  all for non-white people so both of  those things seemed to me to be  something we needed to tackle  of the more than  1160 notable people who are name checked  on the plaques 96 percent are white  while only 4 percent have been from  black asian or minority ethnic  backgrounds something english heritage  says it's working hard to change given  london's ancient and current history of  migration as well as britain's colonial  past  i was very excited when i found out  about it american julia ellen kraft  davis is a direct descendant of the  latest figures to be immortalized with a  blue plaque outside this modest house in  west london  her great great grandparents william and  ellen kraft  the crafts had been slaves in georgia  before escaping to britain in 1850 where  they became prominent abolitionists but  it's how they escaped which to this day  continues to amaze the fair-skinned  ellen posed as a disabled white man and  william as his servant  what did it feel like when you first saw  that blue plaque and saw their names  well it was  it was special i was really  thankful  davis says the craft's story which also  saw them learn how to read and write  become teachers publish a widely  acclaimed book called running a thousand  miles for freedom and purchasing their  own home  needs to be remembered our young people  need to see this because this is how  they  consider you know the class went through  a lot to be to be free  and so that this is to help the young  people understand it may not be easy you  may not have a lot of money  but you know you still need to go ahead  and do the best you can  the crafts had several children before  moving back to the u.s after the 13th  amendment abolishing slavery was  ratified but some of their descendants  still live in britain your dna my dna  like davis cousin chris clark who along  with other family members attended the  unveiling of the blue plaque together  what does it feel like to have this  history  here in the uk so far away from me isn't  that something  william and ellen kraft figures from the  past whose names won't soon be forgotten  for cbs saturday morning empty house  tayyip london  i love those plaques when you walk  around and you see those and it's nice  to see now you start having to pay  attention to other people that you  didn't know before yeah and i never  realized she posed as a disabled  man and and he and her husband were used  as servants because she was so fair she  could pass in shameless promotion here i  have a piece on sunday morning tomorrow  on the subject of passing so be sure to  watch this
 

